<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raynald of Châtillon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1125–<a href="/years/1187">1187</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Reynald of Châtillon · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-chatillon">House of Châtillon</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raynald of Châtillon was a French knight who became Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> by marriage in 1153 and, after years as a captive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, Lord of Oultrejordain through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephanie-of-milly">Stephanie of Milly</a> in 1176. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> he raided Muslim caravans and in 1182–1183 sent ships into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, provoking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. Captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> in 1187, he was executed by Saladin himself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon.jpg" alt="Raynald of Châtillon" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ReynaldofChatillon%26PatriarchofAntioch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raynald was the younger son of Hervé II, Lord of Donzy, a family of influential noblemen in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. He inherited the lordship of Châtillon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, and later complained to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> that part of his patrimony had been violently and unjustly confiscated. He came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> in Louis's army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> in 1147 and stayed behind, and in early 1153 he fought at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin III of Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before that siege ended he became engaged to Princess Constance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, a widow. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, his political opponent, called him a kind of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> knight, and her subjects were astonished at the match. Short of money, Raynald arrested and tortured Aimery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a>, the Latin Patriarch of Antioch, in 1154 after Aimery refused him a subsidy. In 1160 or 1161 the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> captured him at Marash, and he spent fifteen years in prison.</p><p>He was released in 1176 for a ransom of 120,000 gold dinars, most probably paid by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>. Constance had died, so he had no claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephanie-of-milly">Stephanie of Milly</a>, heiress of Transjordan, and Baldwin IV also granted him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and Montréal he controlled the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and he was the only Christian leader to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in the 1180s, raiding caravans as far as Tabuk. Historians debate whether he sought booty or meant to stop Saladin annexing new territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late 1186 or early 1187 Raynald attacked a caravan despite a truce and refused compensation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> pillaged his lands for a month and crushed the crusaders at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187. Raynald was captured, and Saladin personally beheaded him after he refused to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Most of what is known of him was recorded by hostile Muslim authors such as Baha ad-Din ibn Shaddad, and Christian writers followed William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Many modern historians see an irresponsible adventurer, while Bernard Hamilton holds that he alone tried to stop Saladin unifying the neighbouring Muslim states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raynald of Châtillon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their fame rested on war with the Crusader states: Zengi's capture of Edessa in 1144, Nur ad-Din's victory at Inab in 1149 and the capture of <b>Raynald of Châtillon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Having received the submission of <b>Raynald of Châtillon</b>, Prince of Antioch, the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos rode through Antioch in a grand ceremonial procession.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The young King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem, a leper since childhood, with a small force of knights including <b>Raynald of Châtillon</b>, surprised and routed Saladin's much larger army near Ramla.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-25">25 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It passed to Philip of Milly and then, through the heiress Stephanie of Milly, to <b>Raynald of Chatillon</b> in 1176.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-oultrejordain">Lordship of Oultrejordain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of France. Died in Hittin, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.81,35.45&amp;year=1165" aria-label="Hittin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Raynald of Châtillon"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5391 4751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5391 4751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.81,35.45&amp;year=1165">What was here in 1165? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
